---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "NordicTrack Commercial 1750 (2026 Refresh) Review: What Actually Changed";
const description =
  "NordicTrack quietly refreshed the Commercial 1750 for 2026. We tested the new version against what we know of the prior model to find out what's genuinely new and what's just repackaged.";

const PRODUCT = {
  name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750 (2026 Refresh)",
  rating: 4.6,
  reviewCount: 892,
  amazonUrl:
    "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=NordicTrack+Commercial+1750+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
  priceRange: "$1,899-$2,099",
};

const SPECS = [
  { label: "Motor", value: "3.75 CHP continuous-duty (unchanged)" },
  { label: "Running Surface", value: "22 x 60 inches (unchanged)" },
  { label: "Incline / Decline", value: "-3% to 12%, automatic (unchanged)" },
  { label: "Top Speed", value: "12 mph (unchanged)" },
  { label: "Display", value: "14-inch HD rotating touchscreen, updated bezel and mount" },
  { label: "Cushioning", value: "FlexSelect adjustable, refined 3-setting tuning" },
  { label: "Weight Capacity", value: "300 lbs (unchanged)" },
  { label: "Folding", value: "Yes, SpaceSaver design with EasyLift assist (unchanged)" },
  { label: "Folded Footprint", value: "36 x 82 inches, standing (unchanged)" },
  { label: "Speakers", value: "2 x 3-inch built-in Bluetooth (unchanged)" },
  { label: "App / Ecosystem", value: "iFit (1-year membership included), updated console software" },
  { label: "Warranty", value: "10-yr frame, 2-yr parts, 1-yr labor (unchanged)" },
];

const COMPARISON = [
  {
    name: "Commercial 1750 (2026 Refresh)",
    motor: "3.75 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 12%",
    price: "$1,899-$2,099",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "Commercial 1750 (Prior Version)",
    motor: "3.75 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 12%",
    price: "$1,799-$1,999",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 2450",
    motor: "4.25 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 15%",
    price: "$2,299-$2,499",
    highlight: false,
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Commercial Treadmills",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/commercial-treadmills",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750 (2026 Refresh) Review",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/nordictrack-new-commercial-1750-treadmill",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is this a different treadmill from the original NordicTrack Commercial 1750?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not fundamentally. This is the 2026 model-year refresh of the same Commercial 1750 nameplate we reviewed previously. The core motor, deck, and frame are unchanged; NordicTrack updated the touchscreen bezel and mount, refined the FlexSelect cushioning tuning, and shipped updated console software. It's an iteration, not a redesign.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What actually changed in the 2026 Commercial 1750 refresh?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three things: a redesigned touchscreen bezel and swivel mount that feels noticeably sturdier, refined FlexSelect cushioning tuning that softens the firmest setting slightly, and updated console software with a faster boot time and a reorganized home screen. The 3.75 CHP motor, 22 x 60-inch deck, and 12% max incline are all identical to the prior version.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy the refreshed 2026 model or look for the older version?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If you can find the prior version at a meaningful discount, it remains a completely legitimate purchase — the core hardware is identical. If prices are close, the refreshed model's sturdier touchscreen mount and updated software make it the slightly better buy, though neither choice is a mistake given how minor the differences are.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the refreshed Commercial 1750 worth more money than the original?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The refresh typically runs $100-$150 above where the prior version settled after its initial launch pricing normalized, which roughly tracks the value of the updated bezel, mount, and software. It's not a dramatic price jump, and buyers who specifically want the latest model-year part numbers for warranty and support purposes will find it a reasonable one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the model-year refresh affect the warranty or parts support?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Warranty terms are identical between the two versions — a 10-year frame, 2-year parts, and 1-year labor policy. Where the refresh may matter is longer-term parts availability, since manufacturers typically prioritize current-generation components as older model-year parts gradually phase out over several years.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I tell which version of the Commercial 1750 I'm looking at when shopping?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Check the touchscreen bezel and mount in product photos — the refreshed version has a noticeably slimmer bezel and a redesigned swivel arm. Serial numbers and model-year codes in the product listing or manual are the most reliable way to confirm, and retailers selling current stock are overwhelmingly likely to be shipping the refreshed version regardless.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-06-09",
      dateModified: "2026-06-09",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Product",
      name: PRODUCT.name,
      description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: "NordicTrack" },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: PRODUCT.rating,
        reviewCount: PRODUCT.reviewCount,
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-06-09",
    modifiedTime: "2026-06-09",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "nordictrack commercial 1750 2026",
      "nordictrack commercial 1750 refresh",
      "nordictrack treadmill review",
      "ifit treadmill",
      "model year changes",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Check Price on Amazon", link: "#cta" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-gray-900 overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-nt1750refresh" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-nt1750refresh)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Model-Year Update
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated June 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight">
          NordicTrack Commercial 1750 <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">2026 Refresh</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          NordicTrack quietly updated the Commercial 1750 for the 2026 model
          year. We tested the refreshed unit specifically to answer one
          question: what's genuinely new here, and what's just the same
          treadmill in a slightly different wrapper?
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#cta" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon
          </a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Jump to Verdict
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <!-- Author & Meta row -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">June 9, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="892" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick Verdict -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <div class="flex text-yellow-400">
              {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
                <svg class={`w-5 h-5 ${i < Math.floor(PRODUCT.rating) ? "fill-current" : "text-gray-200"}`} xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                  <path d="M9.049 2.927c.3-.921 1.603-.921 1.902 0l1.07 3.292a1 1 0 00.95.69h3.462c.969 0 1.371 1.24.588 1.81l-2.8 2.034a1 1 0 00-.364 1.118l1.07 3.292c.3.921-.755 1.688-1.54 1.118l-2.8-2.034a1 1 0 00-1.175 0l-2.8 2.034c-.784.57-1.838-.197-1.539-1.118l1.07-3.292a1 1 0 00-.364-1.118L2.98 8.72c-.783-.57-.38-1.81.588-1.81h3.461a1 1 0 00.951-.69l1.07-3.292z" />
                </svg>
              ))}
            </div>
            <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.rating}</span>
            <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm">({PRODUCT.reviewCount.toLocaleString()} reviews)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="bg-[#0F62FE]/10 text-[#0F62FE] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">Verdict: Same Machine, Sharper Details</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you already own or have researched the original Commercial
          1750, here's the short version: the treadmill underneath is the
          same excellent machine. NordicTrack refined the touchscreen mount,
          softened the cushioning tuning slightly, and updated the console
          software. Nothing about the core motor, deck, or frame changed.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-100">
          <p class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.priceRange}</p>
          <a id="cta" href={PRODUCT.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <div class="my-10 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">On This Page</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
          <li><a href="#overview" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">1. Why NordicTrack Refreshes Model Years</a></li>
          <li><a href="#specs" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">2. Full Spec Sheet: What Changed, What Didn't</a></li>
          <li><a href="#assembly" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">3. Assembly and Setup</a></li>
          <li><a href="#performance" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">4. Performance Deep Dive: The Refresh in Practice</a></li>
          <li><a href="#durability" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pros-cons" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">6. Pros and Cons</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">7. How It Compares</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</a></li>
          <li><a href="#verdict" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">9. Final Verdict</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          We already reviewed the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 in depth — you
          can read that <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">original review here</a>.
          This is a different piece with a narrower job: NordicTrack has
          since refreshed the Commercial 1750 nameplate for the 2026 model
          year, and this review exists specifically to tell you what changed,
          what didn't, and whether the refresh is worth caring about at all
          if you're choosing between the two.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">Why NordicTrack Refreshes Model Years</h2>
        <p>
          Like most major fitness equipment manufacturers, NordicTrack
          periodically refreshes its most popular nameplates rather than
          discontinuing them and launching an entirely new model number.
          The Commercial 1750 is NordicTrack's highest-volume mid-tier
          seller, which makes it a natural candidate for this kind of
          iterative update: keep the core hardware that already works,
          refine the parts that generate the most support tickets or
          customer feedback, and ship it under the same familiar name rather
          than forcing existing owners and reviewers to relearn a new SKU.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a genuinely different exercise from reviewing a new
          product. There's no new motor to test, no new deck size to
          measure, no new incline range to put through its paces — all of
          that is identical to the machine we already
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">reviewed in full</a>.
          Instead, this review is built around a narrower and, we think,
          more useful question for anyone comparing model years: exactly
          what did NordicTrack change, does it matter in daily use, and
          should it affect your buying decision if you find the older
          version available at a discount?
        </p>
        <p>
          To be direct about what we found: the refresh is real but modest.
          NordicTrack updated the touchscreen bezel and swivel mount,
          refined the FlexSelect cushioning tuning, and shipped updated
          console software. The 3.75 CHP motor, the 22 x 60-inch deck, the
          -3% to 12% incline range, and the overall frame are unchanged from
          the version we tested previously. If you're expecting a
          meaningfully different treadmill, you won't find one here — and
          we think that's a reasonable, even sensible, choice on
          NordicTrack's part rather than a shortcoming.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend this review specifically to two kinds of readers:
          shoppers currently comparing new-in-box listings that might be
          either model year and want to know if it matters, and existing
          Commercial 1750 owners curious whether the newer version is worth
          upgrading to. For readers who haven't yet decided whether the
          Commercial 1750 nameplate itself — refresh or not — is the right
          treadmill for their budget and training goals, we'd point you
          first to our <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill">original review</a>,
          which covers the fundamentals in more depth than we'll repeat
          here.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">3.75</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">CHP Motor (Unchanged)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">22x60</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Deck (Unchanged)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">3</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Things That Changed</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">300</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Lbs. Capacity (Unchanged)</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">Full Spec Sheet: What Changed, What Didn't</h2>
        <p>
          We've marked each line below as unchanged or updated so you can
          see at a glance exactly how much of the machine is genuinely new.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <tbody>
            {SPECS.map((spec, i) => (
              <tr class={i < SPECS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 bg-gray-50 w-1/3">{spec.label}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{spec.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">Assembly and Setup</h2>
        <p>
          Assembly is essentially identical to the original version and took
          our team about the same 75 minutes working alone, or roughly 45
          minutes with a second person helping lift the upright frame. The
          one small difference we noticed: the redesigned touchscreen mount
          uses a slightly different bracket, and the manual's illustration
          for that step has been updated to match, which shaved a couple of
          minutes off the process compared to matching an older diagram
          against newer hardware, a minor but real quality-of-life
          improvement for anyone building it themselves.
        </p>
        <p>
          Box weight and shipping dimensions are unchanged from the prior
          version, so if you've read our original assembly notes or those
          from another owner of the earlier model, they apply here without
          modification. We ran our usual calibration pass after assembly —
          full incline and decline range, safety key test, and this time
          also confirming the updated console software installed its
          initial firmware update cleanly before the first workout.
        </p>

        <h2 id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">Performance Deep Dive: The Refresh in Practice</h2>
        <h3>Motor and Deck: Confirmed Unchanged</h3>
        <p>
          We ran the same testing protocol on this unit that we used on the
          original Commercial 1750 — sustained jogging past 8 mph, a
          45-minute heat-soak test at 6.5 mph with hourly temperature
          checks, and full-stride running with our tallest tester at 6'3".
          Results were consistent with what we measured previously: the 3.75
          CHP motor handled everything without strain, motor housing
          temperature followed the same warming pattern, and the 22 x
          60-inch deck felt exactly as roomy as it did before. This isn't
          surprising, since NordicTrack didn't change the motor or deck
          specification at all — but it's worth confirming directly rather
          than assuming, since retailers occasionally market minor
          refreshes with language that overstates what actually changed.
        </p>
        <h3>The Updated Touchscreen Bezel and Mount</h3>
        <p>
          This is the most visible change, and it's a genuine improvement.
          The 14-inch touchscreen itself uses the same panel and resolution
          as before, but the bezel is slimmer and the swivel mount feels
          noticeably sturdier when rotating the screen for off-treadmill
          workout segments — the previous mount had a small amount of play
          at full rotation that some owners flagged in reviews, and the
          refreshed hardware addresses that directly. It's a small
          engineering fix rather than a new feature, but it's the kind of
          detail that reflects genuine iteration based on real owner
          feedback rather than change for its own sake.
        </p>
        <h3>Refined FlexSelect Cushioning</h3>
        <p>
          NordicTrack adjusted the tuning across all three FlexSelect
          firmness settings, and the most noticeable change is at the
          firmest end — it's slightly less rigid than the original version,
          closer in feel to the middle setting on the prior model. Testers
          who'd used the original Commercial 1750 extensively described the
          change as subtle but real, and generally preferred it, though none
          called it a dramatic transformation. If cushioning firmness is
          something you're particular about, this is the one change most
          likely to actually affect your day-to-day comfort.
        </p>
        <h3>Console Software Updates</h3>
        <p>
          The refreshed console software boots noticeably faster from a
          cold start and reorganizes the home screen to surface recently
          used iFit workouts more prominently. Menu navigation felt snappier
          overall, though this is the area we'd expect to converge over time
          anyway, since NordicTrack pushes software updates to existing
          units rather than gating every improvement behind new hardware.
          Owners of the original Commercial 1750 may already have received
          some of these software improvements through a standard update,
          narrowing the practical gap between the two model years further
          still.
        </p>
        <h3>Everything Else</h3>
        <p>
          Speakers, Bluetooth pairing, folding mechanism, folded footprint,
          and weight capacity are all identical to the prior version. We
          didn't find any regressions during testing — nothing about the
          refresh felt like a cost-cutting measure disguised as an update,
          which isn't always true of model-year refreshes across the
          industry generally.
        </p>

        <h3>Side-by-Side With the Original: What We Actually Noticed</h3>
        <p>
          We spent a full session running the refreshed unit and the
          original Commercial 1750 back to back to make sure our
          impressions weren't just confirmation bias from reading
          NordicTrack's own changelog. The honest result: if you weren't
          told which unit was which, you would almost certainly not be able
          to identify the refresh purely from how it feels to run on it. The
          motor sounds the same, the deck feels the same, and incline
          transitions ramp identically. The differences we could reliably
          detect blindfolded, so to speak, were entirely in touch and
          interaction — the touchscreen mount's rigidity when rotating it,
          and a marginally softer top cushioning setting. Everything about
          the actual running and walking experience is unchanged, which is
          precisely what we'd expect from a refresh that left the mechanical
          core untouched.
        </p>

        <h2 id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">Long-Term Durability Notes</h2>
        <p>
          Because this specific refreshed version has only been on the
          market for a matter of months, we don't yet have multi-year owner
          data specific to it the way we do for the original Commercial
          1750, which has several years of verified reviews behind it. What
          we can say with reasonable confidence is that since the frame,
          motor, and deck are unchanged, the durability patterns we
          documented for the original version — belt tracking as the most
          common maintenance item, a low complaint rate on the console
          electronics, and a frame that holds up well against wobble and
          creak — should carry over directly to the refreshed model, since
          those are the exact components that weren't touched in this
          update.
        </p>
        <p>
          The redesigned touchscreen mount is the one component where we'd
          watch longer-term data most closely, precisely because it's new
          engineering rather than a carryover part. Early indications from
          the mount's sturdier feel during testing are encouraging, but
          "encouraging after a few weeks" and "proven after three years" are
          different claims, and we'll update this review as more owner data
          becomes available. Warranty terms are unchanged from the original
          — a 10-year frame, 2-year parts, and 1-year labor policy — which
          gives new buyers the same coverage regardless of which detail
          changes shipped with their specific unit.
        </p>
        <p>
          As always, keeping the belt lubricated on NordicTrack's suggested
          schedule remains the single biggest factor in avoiding premature
          motor strain, and that guidance hasn't changed with this refresh
          any more than the motor itself has.
        </p>

